WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
You ever notice how it seems like every popular franchise has a character who doesn't talk?

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.940
Or barely talks?
[Mario: Lets-a go!]

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.560
And how they are always so beloved??

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:09.540
Snoopy? Check.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.420
Kenny from South Park? Check.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.640
Chewbacca and R2-D2? Double check.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:14.420
[BB-8 noise]

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:17.200
Pikachu, Groot, Hodor for crying out loud!

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.040
Check, check, and screw you Bran!

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.460
So you know what, guys? I'm just gonna do a bit of a rebrand.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:28.940
From now on, I'm gonna limit myself to saying five things: MatPat, theory, YouTube, Diet Coke, and subscribe.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:34.420
YouTube! MatPat. Subscribe? Theory!! [sigh] Diet Coke.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.080
Aren't I adorable now?

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:52.340
[theme plays]

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.060
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:00.780
The show that speaks the true universal language, complaining about movies on the internet.

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.700
Today though, I'm not complaining about some cinematic stinker,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.340
or even ruining a childhood classic.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.640
No, today I'm talking "Guardians of the Galaxy",

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.520
Which is one of my, if not my all time favourite slice of the MCU pie.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.280
Wait. you didn't know I that loved these movies?

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.220
Didn't you see the last "Guardians of the Galaxy" ep - [record scratch]

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.680
Huh. I haven't done a "Guardians of the Galaxy" episode.

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.960
Maybe it means that even from my cold little dead Theorist heart,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.960
There are some corners of moviedom that remain sacred.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.520
Well at least until today, and hey,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.660
better late than never, right?

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.400
Chris Pratt, I'd never forget about you!

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.640
And you know a movie's good when you have

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:36.663
a Pratt-tastic lead,

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.220
a deadpan beserker who takes everything literally,

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.220
and a smack talking trash panda,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.140
and none of them come out as the fan favourite of the movie,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:45.572
because as we all know,

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:48.560
the fan favourite of Guardians is clearly Groot.

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.040
He's the Guardian that we all aspire to be;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.080
Eco-friendly,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:53.320
Laid back,

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.000
Tree-person of few words.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:58.100
And it's that last part that I wanna figure out today.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:01.220
Groot famously says only three words

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:06.580
"I AM GROOT!"

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.580
Except when he doesn't at the end of the first movie,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.580
(Spoilers from five years ago.)

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.980
"We...are...Groot."

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.532
And yet, we're supposed to believe

00:02:16.532 --> 00:02:18.220
that this is a language, all it's own.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:21.720
Rocket Raccoon doesn't have any trouble understanding Groot's meaning.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.740
"I am Groot."

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:23.340
"Uh-huh."

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:24.760
"I am Groot..."

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.580
"That's right."

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.580
He's not just humoring the old sap,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.580
BA-DUM-TSH!

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.260
Thor says in Infinity War that he took a class on Groot in Asgard

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.680
"You speak Groot?"

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.360
"Yes, they taught it on Asgard."

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.580
So we know that this language is not just specific to this one Groot,

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:42.780
nor is it only understandable to someone who has been around Groot for a very long time.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.920
Which is how I first assumed it worked when I saw the first "Guardians" movie,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.340
So anyway, our question today hinges on the reality of a language built around three words;

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.340
I am Groot.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.807
Would it be possible to have a workable language

00:02:56.807 --> 00:03:00.560
where you could actually communicate meaningfully with those three words?

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.180
Would it be possible for a human to understand it?

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.040
And could we, in the real world, learn Groot?

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.700
We're going well beyond Rosetta Stone, Duolingo and Babbel today, kids.

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:12.700
We're talking about talking to trees!

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.700
And not the kind that'll carry you to Isengard.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.920
We can determine how feasible Groot's language is in real life from a few different angles,

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.920
But they all require just a little bit of knowledge of how languages work

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:24.480
and actually give meaning to everything around us.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.960
So obviously vocabulary is one big way that we get meaning.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:32.120
We assign different sounds to be associated with different objects or ideas,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.120
keeps everything pretty straight forward.

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:37.120
It's hardly the only way to tell one object from another.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.100
All the ways that we can assign meaning to things around us in language

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.100
is called linguistic prosody.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:45.660
Prosody refers to the properties of syllables, words and sentences,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:48.060
that can change the meaning of the overall message.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:52.040
Sure, we can change the words we use and that changes the message we're delivering,

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.658
But there are lots of other ways to change the message

00:03:54.658 --> 00:03:56.500
that most of us never even think about.

00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:00.000
While there is no one standardized list of every category in prosody,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.220
The four major elements are pitch, length, prominence and timbre.

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:08.800
Pitch, just like in singing, has to do with the tone or note of how you say a word or phrase.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:10.756
In English, it isn't so much the specific note

00:04:10.756 --> 00:04:12.500
with which the word is said that changes,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.920
like different keys on a piano produce different notes,

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:18.060
but more about how the movement of pitch creates different meanings.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.700
If I say the phrase, "You don't like Cheetos"

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:22.700
that kind of flat, even tone,

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:27.780
you know I'm just saying that you're not into the delicious puffs of processed cheesy deliciousness.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.780
But then, without changing any words,

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:31.800
I can say the exact same sentence,

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.760
and move the pitch upward at the end,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:34.940
to make it a question.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.220
You don't like Cheetos?

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.920
Asking whether you like them,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.980
and clearly implying that you have no taste in snack food.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:44.140
In other languages, pitch can change the meanings of the same word entirely.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:47.100
The best known example is probably Mandarin Chinese,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.480
Which is a tonal language and has five distinct tones.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:53.280
It has a reputation as one of the hardest languages to learn,

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.280
particularly for Americans.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.410
In Mandarin, the word "ma" can mean

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:59.620
"mother", "scold", "horse" or "hemp".

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:04.340
And figuring out which one is which depends on identifying exactly which tone is being used.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:08.920
Since I'd probably butcher the pronunciation and get dragged all over the internet for messing it up,

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:10.760
I'm gonna let this lady demonstrate.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:11.640
"Ma"

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:12.320
"Ma"

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.000
"Ma-a"

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:14.800
"Ma"

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.620
Lots of languages allow for tonal variation,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.220
usually just with a high, medium or low tone,

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.940
but there are some exceptions.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:23.940
Wobe, a language spoken in Cote D'Ivoire,

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:27.340
has as many as fourteen different tones.

00:05:27.340 --> 00:05:29.700
Now, how does all this relate back to Groot?

00:05:29.700 --> 00:05:33.180
Well, despite the fact that Groot is only using three words,

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:39.960
tonal variations on those words could very easily give us anywhere from three to fourteen separate meanings,

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.862
and then, combining those tones together

00:05:41.862 --> 00:05:45.000
could generate a huge variety of meanings from their combinations,

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:47.000
and that's just the beginning.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:50.620
Length is the next type of prosody, and it's pretty self explanatory.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.060
How long a sound is can affect how it's interpreted.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:56.260
For example, you're more likely to take me seriously if I say "Hey.",

00:05:56.260 --> 00:05:58.260
than if I say "Haaaay!".

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:03.340
The elongation of the second one in English tends to imply that I'm either a little drunk or I'm coming onto you.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:06.780
But any elongation or abbreviation of the way that we say a word in English

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.840
isn't gonna change what the word fundamentally means,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.160
just how we interpret it.

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:16.760
But in other languages, the length can absolutely change the literal interpretation of the words as well.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.100
With one of the best examples being Morse code.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:23.120
In Morse code, sequences of short and long beeps, known as "dots" and "dashes",

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:25.720
are combined to form letters that eventually spell out a message.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.752
Now, you're not gonna get a lot out of the phrase "I am Groot"

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.660
if you're thinking in Morse code terms,

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.000
That's only three beats.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:37.640
And it takes a full eighteen beats in Morse code just to say "Diet Coke" without even adding #NotSponsored.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.631
On the other hand, it would be perfectly feasible

00:06:39.631 --> 00:06:41.500
that there is a difference in meaning between;

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:42.420
"I am Groot."

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:47.820
and "I AM GROOT!"

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.587
While it's hard to measure the lengths of words exactly,

00:06:50.587 --> 00:06:52.020
you can use music as a proxy.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:56.820
And at least say that you can compare the length of words to a whole note, half note, quarter, eighth or sixteenth,

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:58.820
and that gives you a lot more room to work with.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.278
So as long as Groot can keep time by tapping his...

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.300
I don't know, he doesn't have toes, his... roots, I guess?

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:08.220
He can get a lot of different rhythmic variations out of that single catchphrase.

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.200
We'll add how many different variations to the calculation in just a minute.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.896
The next bit of prosody for us to consider is the one I experience

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:17.860
every time I get a phone call from a number that isn't saved into my phone: Stress.

00:07:17.860 --> 00:07:19.860
Or even when the number is saved into my phone.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:20.900
It's a phone, I don't wanna talk to you!

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:24.340
Also known as accent, or emphasis, or prominence,

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.680
ironically, linguistics can't make up it's mind about what it's wants to call it's own terms.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.080
Anyway, the meanings of phrases and sentences can change dramatically

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.020
when we either stress, or don't stress a certain part.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.100
My favourite example is actually a meme that's melted the brains of redditors for years.

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:45.440
The sentence "I never said she stole my money." has vastly different connotations when you stress each different word.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.800
"*I* never said she stole my money." Indicates that somebody else accused her of being a thief.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:55.520
"I never *said* she stole my money." Implies that you think she probably jacked your wallet, but you weren't going public with that.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:01.060
"I never said she stole my *money*." Means that she stole your bike, or your heart, or your Netflix password.

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:02.500
And so on, and so on, and so on.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:05.800
This one is pretty binary, in that you can stress a word or you can leave it un-stressed,

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.260
but that still multiplies the number of ways Groot can say "I am Groot." by a factor of eight.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:11.260
"*I* am Groot."

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:12.320
"I *am* Groot."

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:13.100
"I am *Groot*."

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:14.160
"*I am* Groot."

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:14.880
"*I* am *Groot*."

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:15.560
"I *am Groot*.",

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:17.240
"*I am Groot*.",

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:17.960
"I am Groot."

00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:19.640
Eight. Eight different varieties.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.940
Thankyou ladies and gentlemen, Vin Diesel eat your heart out.

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:24.760
And the last bit of prosody we're discussing is timbre.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:27.600
Talking timbre of a talking timber, am I right?!

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.620
Hoho, the dad jokes are just coursing through my veins today!

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:33.480
Timbre is the hardest of the prosodic elements to define,

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:34.960
because it's pretty subjective.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.520
But in layman's terms it's the sound quality that's being produced.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.810
This can get super technical if you start using terms like

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.760
"Microintonation", "Prefix" and "Spectral envelope".

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.560
But we all experience timbre in ways that are easier to describe, like vibrato.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:51.300
There's certainly a difference between saying "Groot" in a normal voice,

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:53.920
and modulating it to "Grooooot".

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.780
And then, you've got things that are hard to quantify, but easy to differentiate, like sound quality.

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:02.260
Like in Dumb and Dumber, Jim Carrey's most annoying sound in the world...

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:05.080
*most annoying sound in the world*

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.160
Okay, okay, cut it. That's enough.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.354
The most annoying sound in the world is just a note,

00:09:08.354 --> 00:09:11.074
that is obviously gonna sound a lot different from the same note

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:15.540
played for the same length, at the same volume, from say, an Oboe.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:17.420
*oboe noise*

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:21.360
One sounds like music, and sounds like a goose being shoved through a paper shredder.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:25.000
Fun fact, in college I took a voice class that focused heavily on timbre.

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:28.940
Our professor made us develop a voice called the "hollow wood voice".

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.620
I literally spent a semester trying to talk like hollow wood.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.240
Also "metal voice", metal voice. There it is.

00:09:35.240 --> 00:09:37.080
That's what I did for an entire semester.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.600
Ladies and gentlemen, the life of a theater major.

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.540
Ironically enough, even with a name like "hollow wood", I am still no better at translating Groot.

00:09:43.540 --> 00:09:44.620
What a waste.

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:45.560
(that was "hollow wood".)

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:47.940
This is actually part "hollow wood", part "broken earth".

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.180
Putting that college education to use!

00:09:50.180 --> 00:09:52.420
Now, from here we can keep going down the old timbre road,

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:56.400
if we were to include things like coloration and sound quality and vibrato,

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:59.620
at which point the permutations of "I am Groot" would be infinite.

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:04.060
But timbre rarely affects the meaning of language, it more affects the experience of it.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:07.640
Morgan Freeman's voice doesn't fundamentally change what's being said,

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.840
it just makes it more soothing. And buttery...

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:10.760
"What does the fox say?"

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:11.820
"A ring-a-ding-ding."

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.020
I don't know what it is with Morgan Freeman and my episodes lately,

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:15.340
but mmm! Like butter!

00:10:15.340 --> 00:10:17.340
Therefore, I'm gonna leave timbre out the calculations,

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.880
though Groot's timbre would definitely fluctuate based on his mood.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:22.320
"I am Groot."

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:23.820
"I am Groot."

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.580
"I AM GROOT."

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:31.580
So, is there enough variation in the way Groot can say those three magic words to make it an official language?

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:33.780
Well, let's imagine a best-base scenario.

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:37.480
If we say that Groot's language has as many notes as Wobe's fourteen,

00:10:37.480 --> 00:10:43.380
that length can be distinguished into whole, half, quarter, eighth and sixteenth notes to create different meanings,

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:46.740
and that the meaning changes when a word is stressed or unstressed,

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:49.960
well, we get a lot of ways of saying that phrase.

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.280
Two million, seven-hundred and forty-four thousand ways to be exact.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:56.280
Maybe that's being a bit too optimistic though,

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.100
so if we keep the tones,

00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.980
but instead of using all those differentiations in length,

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.380
then instead focus on whether the word is short or long,

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:03.380
like in Morse code,

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:09.340
we still end up with one-hundred and seventy-five thousand, six-hundred and sixteen different variations

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:10.900
of "I am Groot."

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:15.000
For comparison, the second edition of the Oxford English Dictionary

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.360
gives the English language a strangely similar number of words,

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:22.080
at one-hundred and seventy-one thousand, four hundred and seventy six.

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.460
So the number of ways that we can say "I am Groot"

00:11:24.460 --> 00:11:27.520
are at the level of the English language.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.300
Not too shabby!

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:32.160
I, for one, am pretty darn happy with those findings.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.940
We have made a solid case for Groot's language here,

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.020
but, I can see you arguing that this can't actually work

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:40.580
because there's no real world language that works this way.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.811
"No language just works with three words,

00:11:42.811 --> 00:11:45.640
and just modifications in tone and rhythm and stuff,

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:47.140
there's no proof.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:49.000
And you know, that's a reasonable complaint.

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.000
Or, at least it would be, if it were true!

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:54.800
The fact is, plenty of languages exist with limited vocabularies.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:59.540
The created language Toki Pona only has a hundred and twenty-three different words,

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:03.160
and while it relies on creating compounds to create more complicated ideas,

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.440
much like German does,

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.260
it's able to express a lot of complex thoughts.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:11.140
"But that's a manufactured language, not a language with any native speakers!"

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.200
Says you, the stickler who should really stop watching my videos,

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:16.760
and just start cashing in on your vast trivia expertise,

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:18.920
Jeopardy! is calling to you, friend.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.917
Alright, so if you only want to count languages

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:25.620
that are native to a specific real world culture somewhere on planet Earth at this moment,

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:30.840
there is a real world language that gives us a new minimum of words in a vocabulary,

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.580
and that number is zero.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:39.320
On the Canary Islands, the native Silbo Gomero language is composed entirely of whistles.

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:40.620
*whistles*

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:42.620
No spoken words whatsoever.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:45.000
That sounds like you probably can't say much with it,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:46.740
but you would be incorrect.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:50.340
*whistles*

00:12:50.340 --> 00:12:56.000
So there you go, Groot's three word vocabulary is three more words than a language actually needs.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.340
Which then by proxy means we could learn to speak Groot, right?

00:12:59.340 --> 00:13:02.220
Fans could gather at Comic-Con like Trekkies do at conventions,

00:13:02.220 --> 00:13:04.580
except instead of speaking Klingon, they'd be speaking Groot!

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:06.160
No you can't!

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:08.253
As much as I would love to see it,

00:13:08.253 --> 00:13:11.700
according to Maximus the Mad in the War of Kings series,

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.240
it's likely impossible.

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:16.460
Groot only sounds like he's repeating his name over and over,

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:17.980
because of his hardened larynx.

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:22.300
and the key to understanding him is to listen to the sigh of breeze beneath it,

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:23.720
the nuance of meaning.

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:27.560
For one, listening for some kind of magical breeze is not really an area of prosody,

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:30.380
but more relevant is the hardened larynx bit.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:32.380
Humans do not have a hardened larynx,

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.680
and it's not worth trying to figure out a way to get one,

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:38.720
because in people, a hardened larynx is actually a serious medical condition.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:40.700
In this condition, called Presbylarynx,

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:44.280
people have incredible difficulty speaking loud enough to be heard easily,

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:48.480
and usually have to find other non-verbal forms of communication like sign language to help.

00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.640
So, at the end of the day, the condition that would allow us to speak Groot,

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:56.760
would actually prevent us, as humans, from speaking any words loud enough to be heard at all.

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.680
Guess we'll just have to stay rooted,

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:01.320
and come to terms with the fact that when it comes to speaking Groot,

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.380
we won't be able to branch out.

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:04.466
But hey!

00:14:04.466 --> 00:14:05.880
I am Groot!

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.700
I am Groot!

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.700
IIII am Groot!

